// StackAlignment — the stack residue EVERY backend in this compiler keeps at a call site.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT LIVED IN `Targets/X64/X64PrologueEpilogue` AND THE arm64 PROLOGUE READ IT FROM THERE.** Maxon's
// top-level name space is flat, so that worked; it was still a leak, and the wrong kind — an ISA-neutral
// fact filed under one ISA, which is exactly the shape that makes the OTHER ISA's reader look accidental.
// Three arm64 sites read it today (`Arm64PrologueEpilogue.insertArm64FrameForFunction`,
// `Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction`, and the green-thread chunks) and none of them is about x64.
//
// ⚠ **ONE NUMBER FOR BOTH ISAs IS A COINCIDENCE OF VALUE, NOT OF REASON, AND IT IS STILL ONE FACT.** Win64
// requires rsp ≡ 0 (mod 16) at a `call`; AAPCS64 requires SP to be 16-byte aligned at every instruction that
// uses it as a base, which is strictly stronger. Both are 16, and a target that wanted another residue would
// need its own name here rather than a second `= 16` somewhere — which is precisely what this file removes.
//
// A misaligned stack is never a compile error. On x64 it is a crash inside kernel32's SSE, arbitrarily far
// from the code that was wrong; on arm64 it is an SP-alignment fault at the first frame access. Both are
// unattributable, which is why the number is stated once.
export let StackFrameAlignment = 16
